# Figma MCP Design-Code Sync Workflow (Eleventy + Sanity)

Use this workflow for incremental feature/page updates. Prioritize rapid iteration, low risk, and consistency with existing code patterns.

## Core Principle

- Keep Figma and code aligned in short loops.
- Reuse existing components, templates, and tokens before creating new ones.
- Avoid redesign-by-rewrite; ship the smallest useful improvement.

## Required Figma MCP Sequence

1. Capture current UI state from code and establish a baseline frame in Figma.
2. Create focused variants in Figma (A/B) for only the section being changed.
3. Pull design context for the selected frame/node and implement in existing code patterns.
4. Sync implementation updates back to the Figma file/page.
5. Mark approved and implemented states explicitly in frame names.

## Frame Lifecycle and Naming

- Use frame names: `Current`, `Proposed A`, `Proposed B`, `Approved`, `Implemented`.
- Mirror code structure in naming (example: `Home/Hero`, `Books/Card Grid`, `Editing/Service Cards`).
- Keep one canonical page for production-ready synced screens.

## Implementation Guardrails

- Prefer existing `web/_includes/layouts` and `web/_includes/components` patterns.
- Keep CSS changes scoped; avoid new one-off systems when existing styles can be extended.
- Preserve semantic HTML and accessibility defaults while implementing design updates.
- If design conflicts with platform constraints, choose platform-native implementation and document tradeoff.

## Design System and Tokens

- Search and reuse existing design system components before creating new ones.
- Reuse existing color/type/spacing conventions from the codebase and Figma variables.
- Keep token usage consistent between Figma and code; avoid ad hoc visual values.

## Quality Checks Before Finalizing

- Confirm implemented UI matches approved frame at key breakpoints.
- Validate interaction states for changed elements (hover/focus/active where relevant).
- Confirm content structure still maps cleanly to Sanity fields.
- Confirm no unrelated refactors were introduced.

## MCP Tool Discipline

- Before any `use_figma` call, load the required Figma use skill first.
- Prefer small, reversible updates instead of large multi-section pushes.
- When uncertain, sync one section at a time and verify before proceeding.
